cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below. It has not been peer reviewed."
title: "The Duchet-Meyniel bound is asymptotically sharp for graphs with independence number two"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Ryan"
    given-names: "Jack"
version: "1.0.0"
license: Apache-2.0
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.24527010"
repository-code: "https://github.com/jackryan225/duchet-meyniel-sharp"
abstract: >-
  Research driven, orchestrated and reviewed by Jack Ryan. Claude (by Anthropic), GPT-6.2 Astra (by OpenAI) and
  Gemini 4 Neon (by Google) collaborated in finding and writing the proofs. The three theorems of
  Challenge/Statement.lean (exists_small_hadwiger, not_linear_cm, not_hc_eps) are formalized in Lean 4.
  Jack Ryan is not a professional mathematician, and no professional mathematician has refereed this work.
  For every c > 0 there are arbitrarily large graphs G with independence number at most 2 in which every
  connected matching has fewer than c|V(G)| edges, and the Hadwiger number of these graphs is less than
  (1/3 + 2c)|V(G)| + 2/3. So the bound h(G) >= |V(G)|/3 of Duchet and Meyniel (1982) for graphs with
  independence number at most 2 is asymptotically sharp, and the weak (linear) connected-matching conjecture of
  Furedi, Gyarfas and Simonyi (2005, Conjecture 1.4) is false. The result is a parametric extension of OpenAI's
  counterexample to Hadwiger's conjecture (github.com/openai/math): the marginal cap 2^1000 of OpenAI's Lean proof
  became 2^(12Q+1) and the threshold m/100 became m/Q. Further consequences in the paper are paper arguments only.
keywords:
  - "Hadwiger's conjecture"
  - "Hadwiger number"
  - "graph minors"
  - "independence number"
  - "connected matching"
  - "Duchet-Meyniel theorem"
  - "claw-free graphs"
  - "Lean 4"
  - "formal verification"
